d-no-ceiling,credit-price-needs-its-unit}.md","description":"Find per-message credit prices and recall memory"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgmBDWfTzoSxGS51SsmSW","requestRef":"1e066be2-f969-4f9f-8c25-bb136266a2d7","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"7c769cf4-3aff-4986-a276-7190d27b3189","timestamp":"2026-11-06T06:10:04.356Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35/src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/first-run-fixes","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
{"parentUuid":"7c769cf4-3aff-4986-a276-7190d27b3189","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4e98f1e7-49ff-445f-823b-aeedc91e43e5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ztaAHYjwjQvniRzn5o8BE","type":"tool_result","content":"(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.cpp\n---\nname: per-chat-cost-estimate\ndescription: \"2026-10-28 pricing work: a \\\"prepare this print\\\" chat costs about $0.80-1.40 on Opus 5.5 or GPT-5.6 Sol, from 21 live journeys; data location, conversion factors, cost drivers.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 57f57a8e-f983-48b3-8718-4f3fae9cc6dd\n  modified: 2026-10-29T01:08:52.122Z\n---\n\nSource data: 21 live in-app journeys (2026-10-19/20, DeepSeek V4 Flash, pictures stripped) in\n`~/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_2012258e-6e8e-4c8c-bb42-733d62a15120/plan-journey-batch-{b,c,d,e}/out/*/harness.log`\n(one `input_tokens= cached_input_tokens= output_tokens=` line per request). Each journey: file opened, 1-3 user messages, agent sets up and slices.\nMedian per chat: 19 requests, 365k input tokens (~80% cached), 8k output; max single request ~47k (64 KiB history trim caps growth).\n\nConversions used (my calibration, one request each): Opus 5.5 tokenizer ~1.4x DeepSeek's (first request 16,523 vs 11,813 via the free count_tokens endpoint);\nprompt+30 tools today = 15,094 Opus tokens per request (13,532 on 2026-10-19).\n\nResult (median / p90 per chat): Opus 5.5 $0.78 / $1.47 as measured, ~$1.05 with double output for thinking, ~$1.40 if the person pauses >5 min twice (cache rewrite);\nGPT-5.6 Sol list $5/$30 $0.75 / $1.23, promo $4/$20 $0.59; Sonnet 5.5 about half of Opus.\nBiggest Opus cost is the uncached ~20% of input billed as 5-minute cache writes ($5/M), not cache reads or output.\n\n**Measured on Opus 5.5, 2026-10-28** (3 chats Kenneth approved, $1.29 total; worktree `.claude/worktrees/opus-cost-batch`, branch claude/opus-cost-batch, uncommitted harness switch `JUSPRIN_JOURNEY_PROVIDER=anthropic`; scripts and logs in its untracked `opus-batch/`): plug quick-check $0.30 (11 requests), bunny figurine $0.43 (12), vase with purpose change and one 6-min pause $0.56 (17). About half the DeepSeek-based estimate: Opus made fewer calls, only ~1% of input was uncached, cache writes were ~70% of the bill, output only ~$0.06-0.11 at default effort. The cache breaks back to the 15,210-token tools+prompt prefix at every turn start after a slice (5-13k rewritten each time). A 6-min pause cost only ~$0.07 extra.\nCause of the reset (investigated 2026-10-28, from logs + code, no body diff): it happens at EVERY turn start, the person's and the slice-finished note's, not only after slices. AgentHost::make_agent_request rebuilds earlier turns as replay entries (text only, no thinking, no images, no workspace tag or skill text, string tool results), so the just-ended turn never matches what was sent in-turn; and AnthropicCodec puts no breakpoint at the end of the replayed history, so even the stable older part isn't read (only tools+system). Waste here: 40k tokens rewritten = ~$0.19 of $1.29 (upper bound). Kenneth chose option 1. Prototype 2026-10-28, uncommitted in the opus-cost-batch worktree: ProviderAgent keeps the finished turn (FinishedTurns) and sends it as TurnTranscript::carried when the host's next history = last history + replay of that turn (same AgentRequest::conversation_id, instructions, tools, host-run call ids; no developer/second user entry; carried <128 KiB). Both codecs render carried turns. Host-level test with a scripted Anthropic transport failed before, passes after; all 471 agent tests pass. Estimated saving ~$0.11 of $1.29 (~9%); a cold-cache rewrite after a pause gets slightly bigger. Measured live 2026-10-28 (Kenneth approved, $3 cap): $1.07 vs $1.29 (plug $0.23, bunny $0.32, vase $0.51); slice-note turn starts now read the whole prior prompt (writes 0.9-1.3k instead of 4.6-12.9k), saving ~$0.15, minus ~$0.03 bigger rewrite after the vase's 6-min pause; the rest of the gap is run variance (fewer requests). Still uncarried: turn 1 after the file-report turn, because the host adds the \"Earlier file-load reports\" developer note at the history's start once no skill fires (~1.3k tokens, <1c). Baseline logs moved to opus-batch/out-baseline. Harness model picker shows gpt-5.4-mini (default_chat_model) though Opus is installed; display only.\n\n**Why:** Kenneth started the pricing strategy from this; rerun on a current build with pictures and a real Anthropic/OpenAI model before quoting firm numbers (needs his batch approval, [[prompt-eval-spend-budget]]).\n**How to apply:** reuse the logs and factors above instead of rescanning session transcripts (that scan took >10 min and was abandoned).\n---\nname: credits-one-kind-no-ceiling\ndescription: \"Kenneth's 2026-11-02 evening pricing decisions - every credit is the same (no Standard-only lot), Workshop Standard costs 0 with no ceiling, balances in credits only with bars grouped by expiry, no New Standard chat\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: c790f253-5882-4425-8ee0-6201d35017ca\n  modified: 2026-11-02T20:22:25.192Z\n---\n\nOn 2026-11-02 evening, after seeing canvas version 73, Kenneth corrected the credits design in four ways:\n\n- Every credit is the same. No Standard-only (\"basic_only\", \"trickle\") lot. Free accounts (not subscribed) get 50 credits a month, expiring one month after grant, that pay for either tier. Not \"everyone\": he told the design session \"the 'everyone gets 50 credits' wording is wrong. I never said that.\" I had told the design session \"yes, everyone\" as my own pick before he answered; never pass my pick on as his. His words: \"let's just give free users 50/mo use-it-or-loose it credits\".\n- Workshop has no ceiling. \"no ceiling. It's probably easier just to say standard messages cost 0 credits\". Nothing about 600 or fair use remains.\n- A balance is one number of credits, never \"N Standard or N Expert messages\" beside it (\"showing a number of messages and credits at the same time is fucking confusing\"). The translation line was my addition in the design doc, never his decision. Bars are grouped only by expiry; bought credits get a bar too (decision 13 closed).\n- \"New Standard chat\" and board 3d are gone, since the case only existed because of the Standard-only lot.\n\nPrices in messages appear only where the person chooses or is stopped: picker options, the stop card for the chat's tier, the pricing page's one sentence, the low-balance notice, and one scale line under the first-month offer.\n\n**Why:** he said he was \"seriously worried that we are not on the same page\" and asked me to restate the plan; the Standard-only lot and the ceiling were placeholders the plan carried without his words behind them.\n\n**How to apply:** before presenting any credits surface, check it against these four. When a plan carries a mechanism (a lot kind, a cap) with no quote from Kenneth behind it, flag it as mine rather than drawing it on every board. Related: [[credit-price-needs-its-unit]], [[usage-against-quota-common-shape]], [[trace-a-rule-to-kenneths-words]].\n\nLater the same evening Kenneth reviewed every mechanism that was mine, not his. Kept: the launch offer, \"About 25 credits left\" (credits, not messages), \"Expiring credits are used first\", the savings switch on by default, the Account page shape, Standard as the default tier after the first month, the v1 welcome-back fill-ins, the print-report form details, the alias ids. Dropped: the reverse savings alert and board 7c, the month-end and 7-day expiry notices, the daily cap on free app turns, the thinking picker on Standard. Expiry: every expiring lot expires one month from the day it was granted, never at a calendar month end. His message was only a keep list, so anything left off it counts as dropped. A session wrongly called the report rules \"neither kept nor dropped\"; he corrected that the same day: \"I thought the clawback are removed, rigth? And why is it still a placeholder?\" The clawback is removed. Asked about 25 credits, three a day and stopping after two invalid reports, he ruled: \"Remove these. only keep the 1st one\". A report earns 25 credits, one per G-code, and a review is only recorded.\n\nRuled later: \"no 50 credits for first month. only 500 initial credits. pack holders also get 50 credits a month.\" The 50 a month goes to every account not on a subscription, starting after the first month; the balance example is 62 + 500 = 562 with no Free credits bar.\n---\nname: credit-price-needs-its-unit\ndescription: \"Kenneth, 2026-11-02, on the tier picker: \\\"1 credit\\\" is very confusing. A price alone doesn't say per what, so labels read \\\"1 credit a message\\\".\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 3eabf4a3-87b6-4471-a4e5-b6fb9c14763a\n  modified: 2026-11-02T04:10:42.227Z\n---\n\nOn \"Basic · 1 credit\" in the picker board, Kenneth: \"1 credit it's very very confusing, right?\"\n\n**Why:** my reading, not confirmed by him: the label doesn't say per message, per chat or per print. He may also mean the word \"credit\" itself, which I asked him about in the comment thread.\n\n**How to apply:** wherever a price stands alone, say what it buys: \"Basic · 1 credit a message\", \"Expert · 5 credits a message\". The design doc's shorter strings are out of date. Related: [[usage-against-quota-common-shape]].","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"9d000f17-3f8a-4f86-a609-120f3be5f983","timestamp":"2026-11-06T06:10:04.527Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.cpp\n---\nname: per-chat-cost-estimate\ndescription: \"2026-10-28 pricing work: a \\\"prepare this print\\\" chat costs about $0.80-1.40 on Opus 5.5 or GPT-5.6 Sol, from 21 live journeys; data location, conversion factors, cost drivers.\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 57f57a8e-f983-48b3-8718-4f3fae9cc6dd\n  modified: 2026-10-29T01:08:52.122Z\n---\n\nSource data: 21 live in-app journeys (2026-10-19/20, DeepSeek V4 Flash, pictures stripped) in\n`~/Library/Application Support/Claude/side-session-notes/local_2012258e-6e8e-4c8c-bb42-733d62a15120/plan-journey-batch-{b,c,d,e}/out/*/harness.log`\n(one `input_tokens= cached_input_tokens= output_tokens=` line per request). Each journey: file opened, 1-3 user messages, agent sets up and slices.\nMedian per chat: 19 requests, 365k input tokens (~80% cached), 8k output; max single request ~47k (64 KiB history trim caps growth).\n\nConversions used (my calibration, one request each): Opus 5.5 tokenizer ~1.4x DeepSeek's (first request 16,523 vs 11,813 via the free count_tokens endpoint);\nprompt+30 tools today = 15,094 Opus tokens per request (13,532 on 2026-10-19).\n\nResult (median / p90 per chat): Opus 5.5 $0.78 / $1.47 as measured, ~$1.05 with double output for thinking, ~$1.40 if the person pauses >5 min twice (cache rewrite);\nGPT-5.6 Sol list $5/$30 $0.75 / $1.23, promo $4/$20 $0.59; Sonnet 5.5 about half of Opus.\nBiggest Opus cost is the uncached ~20% of input billed as 5-minute cache writes ($5/M), not cache reads or output.\n\n**Measured on Opus 5.5, 2026-10-28** (3 chats Kenneth approved, $1.29 total; worktree `.claude/worktrees/opus-cost-batch`, branch claude/opus-cost-batch, uncommitted harness switch `JUSPRIN_JOURNEY_PROVIDER=anthropic`; scripts and logs in its untracked `opus-batch/`): plug quick-check $0.30 (11 requests), bunny figurine $0.43 (12), vase with purpose change and one 6-min pause $0.56 (17). About half the DeepSeek-based estimate: Opus made fewer calls, only ~1% of input was uncached, cache writes were ~70% of the bill, output only ~$0.06-0.11 at default effort. The cache breaks back to the 15,210-token tools+prompt prefix at every turn start after a slice (5-13k rewritten each time). A 6-min pause cost only ~$0.07 extra.\nCause of the reset (investigated 2026-10-28, from logs + code, no body diff): it happens at EVERY turn start, the person's and the slice-finished note's, not only after slices. AgentHost::make_agent_request rebuilds earlier turns as replay entries (text only, no thinking, no images, no workspace tag or skill text, string tool results), so the just-ended turn never matches what was sent in-turn; and AnthropicCodec puts no breakpoint at the end of the replayed history, so even the stable older part isn't read (only tools+system). Waste here: 40k tokens rewritten = ~$0.19 of $1.29 (upper bound). Kenneth chose option 1. Prototype 2026-10-28, uncommitted in the opus-cost-batch worktree: ProviderAgent keeps the finished turn (FinishedTurns) and sends it as TurnTranscript::carried when the host's next history = last history + replay of that turn (same AgentRequest::conversation_id, instructions, tools, host-run call ids; no developer/second user entry; carried <128 KiB). Both codecs render carried turns. Host-level test with a scripted Anthropic transport failed before, passes after; all 471 agent tests pass. Estimated saving ~$0.11 of $1.29 (~9%); a cold-cache rewrite after a pause gets slightly bigger. Measured live 2026-10-28 (Kenneth approved, $3 cap): $1.07 vs $1.29 (plug $0.23, bunny $0.32, vase $0.51); slice-note turn starts now read the whole prior prompt (writes 0.9-1.3k instead of 4.6-12.9k), saving ~$0.15, minus ~$0.03 bigger rewrite after the vase's 6-min pause; the rest of the gap is run variance (fewer requests). Still uncarried: turn 1 after the file-report turn, because the host adds the \"Earlier file-load reports\" developer note at the history's start once no skill fires (~1.3k tokens, <1c). Baseline logs moved to opus-batch/out-baseline. Harness model picker shows gpt-5.4-mini (default_chat_model) though Opus is installed; display only.\n\n**Why:** Kenneth started the pricing strategy from this; rerun on a current build with pictures and a real Anthropic/OpenAI model before quoting firm numbers (needs his batch approval, [[prompt-eval-spend-budget]]).\n**How to apply:** reuse the logs and factors above instead of rescanning session transcripts (that scan took >10 min and was abandoned).\n---\nname: credits-one-kind-no-ceiling\ndescription: \"Kenneth's 2026-11-02 evening pricing decisions - every credit is the same (no Standard-only lot), Workshop Standard costs 0 with no ceiling, balances in credits only with bars grouped by expiry, no New Standard chat\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: c790f253-5882-4425-8ee0-6201d35017ca\n  modified: 2026-11-02T20:22:25.192Z\n---\n\nOn 2026-11-02 evening, after seeing canvas version 73, Kenneth corrected the credits design in four ways:\n\n- Every credit is the same. No Standard-only (\"basic_only\", \"trickle\") lot. Free accounts (not subscribed) get 50 credits a month, expiring one month after grant, that pay for either tier. Not \"everyone\": he told the design session \"the 'everyone gets 50 credits' wording is wrong. I never said that.\" I had told the design session \"yes, everyone\" as my own pick before he answered; never pass my pick on as his. His words: \"let's just give free users 50/mo use-it-or-loose it credits\".\n- Workshop has no ceiling. \"no ceiling. It's probably easier just to say standard messages cost 0 credits\". Nothing about 600 or fair use remains.\n- A balance is one number of credits, never \"N Standard or N Expert messages\" beside it (\"showing a number of messages and credits at the same time is fucking confusing\"). The translation line was my addition in the design doc, never his decision. Bars are grouped only by expiry; bought credits get a bar too (decision 13 closed).\n- \"New Standard chat\" and board 3d are gone, since the case only existed because of the Standard-only lot.\n\nPrices in messages appear only where the person chooses or is stopped: picker options, the stop card for the chat's tier, the pricing page's one sentence, the low-balance notice, and one scale line under the first-month offer.\n\n**Why:** he said he was \"seriously worried that we are not on the same page\" and asked me to restate the plan; the Standard-only lot and the ceiling were placeholders the plan carried without his words behind them.\n\n**How to apply:** before presenting any credits surface, check it against these four. When a plan carries a mechanism (a lot kind, a cap) with no quote from Kenneth behind it, flag it as mine rather than drawing it on every board. Related: [[credit-price-needs-its-unit]], [[usage-against-quota-common-shape]], [[trace-a-rule-to-kenneths-words]].\n\nLater the same evening Kenneth reviewed every mechanism that was mine, not his. Kept: the launch offer, \"About 25 credits left\" (credits, not messages), \"Expiring credits are used first\", the savings switch on by default, the Account page shape, Standard as the default tier after the first month, the v1 welcome-back fill-ins, the print-report form details, the alias ids. Dropped: the reverse savings alert and board 7c, the month-end and 7-day expiry notices, the daily cap on free app turns, the thinking picker on Standard. Expiry: every expiring lot expires one month from the day it was granted, never at a calendar month end. His message was only a keep list, so anything left off it counts as dropped. A session wrongly called the report rules \"neither kept nor dropped\"; he corrected that the same day: \"I thought the clawback are removed, rigth? And why is it still a placeholder?\" The clawback is removed. Asked about 25 credits, three a day and stopping after two invalid reports, he ruled: \"Remove these. only keep the 1st one\". A report earns 25 credits, one per G-code, and a review is only recorded.\n\nRuled later: \"no 50 credits for first month. only 500 initial credits. pack holders also get 50 credits a month.\" The 50 a month goes to every account not on a subscription, starting after the first month; the balance example is 62 + 500 = 562 with no Free credits bar.\n---\nname: credit-price-needs-its-unit\ndescription: \"Kenneth, 2026-11-02, on the tier picker: \\\"1 credit\\\" is very confusing. A price alone doesn't say per what, so labels read \\\"1 credit a message\\\".\"\nmetadata:\n  node_type: memory\n  type: feedback\n  originSessionId: 3eabf4a3-87b6-4471-a4e5-b6fb9c14763a\n  modified: 2026-11-02T04:10:42.227Z\n---\n\nOn \"Basic · 1 credit\" in the picker board, Kenneth: \"1 credit it's very very confusing, right?\"\n\n**Why:** my reading, not confirmed by him: the label doesn't say per message, per chat or per print. He may also mean the word \"credit\" itself, which I asked him about in the comment thread.\n\n**How to apply:** wherever a price stands alone, say what it buys: \"Basic · 1 credit a message\", \"Expert · 5 credits a message\". The design doc's shorter strings are out of date. Related: [[usage-against-quota-common-shape]].","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7c769cf4-3aff-4986-a276-7190d27b3189","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/kenneth/Projects/JusPrin/.claude/worktrees/first-run-flow-f7be35","sessionId":"c868826d-3950-4f0f-9ecd-4a549cdd6983","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"claude/first-run-fixes","slug":"dapper-knitting-moler"}
