{"parentUuid":"0d1fc582-e7e7-4d07-9ae5-78b6bfc48ac0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgmjBxyvGVgVMigdAH5Ko","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The rule is in the project's `AGENTS.md`, under the heading \"Landing a branch on jusprin-newui\" ([AGENTS.md:88](AGENTS.md:88)). Line 90 says:\n\n> Merge a feature branch with `git merge --no-ff`, never a squash.\n\nLine 92 gives the reason: a squash loses the step-by-step commits that `git blame` and `git bisect` need. It also makes any branch the squashed one had merged look unmerged, so that work gets merged again with conflicts. The same line says \"Kenneth chose this on 2026-11-05.\"\n\nI wrote that section myself, in this same session, as commit a2d5550058. You didn't write the words. They come from an exchange where you asked whether to squash for a clean history. I recommended `--no-ff` merges, and you answered \"Okay, go with what you suggested.\" So it's your decision, in my wording.\n\nIt doesn't conflict with your later request to squash my commits into one. That squash produced 34cc536134. The rule is about the next step: bringing a finished branch onto jusprin-newui. There, the branch goes in with one `--no-ff` merge commit, and its own commits stay as they are underneath."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1331,"cache_read_input_tokens":201816,"output_tokens":649,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":260},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1331,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":649,"cache_read_input_tokens":201816,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1331,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1331},"type":"message"}],"speed":"s